as a fancy name, to ordinary streets or ranges of houses.—4. The flat roof of a house, as of Oriental and Spanish houses.—5†. A balcony, or open gallery.

{{smaller block|There is a rowe of pretty little ''tarrasses'' or rayles betwixt every window.
{{right|''Coryat'', Crudities, I. 218.}}}}

{{smaller block|As touching open galleries and ''terraces'', they were devised by the Greekes, who were wont to cover their houses with such.
{{right|''Holland'', tr. of Pliny, xxxvi. 25}}}}

6. In ''marble-working'', a defective spot in marble, which, after being cleaned out, is filled with some artificial preparation. Also ''terrasse''.

'''terrace<sup>1</sup>''' (ter′ās), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''terraced'', ppr. ''terracing''. [< ''terrace'', ''n.''] To form into a terrace; furnish with a terrace.

{{smaller block|Methinks the grove of Baal I see<br>In ''terraced'' stages mount up high.
{{right|''Dyer'', To Aaron Hill.}}}}

'''terrace<sup>2</sup>''' (ter′ās), ''n.'' [Also ''terrass'', ''terrasse'', ''tarrace'', ''tarris'', ''tarras''; = MD. ''terras'', ''tiras'', D. ''tras'', rubbish, brick-dust, = G. ''tarras'', ''trass'', < It. ''terraccia'', rubble, rubbish, < ''terra'', earth: see ''terrace''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''trass''.] A variety of mortar used for pargeting and the like, and for lining kilns for pottery.

{{smaller block|They [the kilns] plastered within with a reddish mortar or ''tarris''.
{{right|''Letter of'' 1677, in Jewitt's Ceramic Art, I. 40.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Tarrace'', or ''Terrace'', a coarse sort of plaister, or mortar, durable in the weather, chiefly used to line basons, cisterns, wells, and other reservoirs of water.
{{right|''Chambers'', Cyclopædia (ed. 1738).}}}}

